package fmt

// Package fmt writes text and bytes to standard output, and to standard
// error. A program imports it as
//
//     import fmt from "std/fmt"
//
// and the compiler reads it from $TACK_HOME/src/std/fmt, or
// ~/.tack/src/std/fmt, as it does every std package. println
// is written in Tack on top of the print builtin; printf's formatting is
// C, in printf.c, and write's output is C, in write.c, which the driver
// compiles and links with every program that imports fmt, as it does any
// package's C files. All three write through C's stdout, as print does,
// so their output interleaves in the order of the calls.
//
// appendf formats as printf does, byte for byte, but appends the output
// to a list[uint8] instead of writing it, a signature like Go's
// fmt.Appendf with printf's output, all or nothing, and returns the builtin append's error when the list cannot
// grow. It is written in Tack, in appendf.tck, with a type switch over
// each any, std/bytes' integer appends and std/conv's append_float, the
// same algorithm as printf.c's floats; printf and eprintf stay in C.
//
// eprintln, eprintf and ewrite are their twins for standard error: each
// does exactly what its twin does, but writes through C's stderr, so
// their output interleaves with each other's in the order of the calls,
// as the stdout ones' does. Each flushes both ways around its write:
// C's stdout first, as C++ ties cerr to cout, and C's stderr after, so
// where both streams reach one pipe, file or terminal, their bytes
// arrive in the order of the calls, however the C library buffers
// either stream. Like print, none reports an error: a closed descriptor
// or a failed write loses the bytes, and a write to a pipe with no
// reader, on either stream, ends the process as exit(141) does, as it
// does for print: every allocator frees every buffer it still holds, no
// defer runs, and the status is 141.

// println writes s to standard output exactly as given, then a newline,
// "\n". println("") writes the newline alone. Like print, it reports no
// error, and a pipe with no reader ends the process as exit(141) does.
pub func println(s string) {
    print(s)
    print("\n")
}

// printf writes format to standard output with each verb in it replaced
// by the next of args, formatted as the verb says, and every other byte
// as it is. A verb is % and the character after it:
//
//     %d  an integer of any type, in decimal, with - before a negative one
//     %x  an integer of any type, in lowercase hexadecimal with no 0x,
//         with - before a negative one: -1 is -1, as in Go, not ff
//     %s  a string, its bytes exactly, or a []uint8, its bytes the same
//         way: printf("undefined: %s\n", name.bytes())
//     %t  a bool, true or false
//     %c  a uint8, the byte itself
//     %g  a float32 or a float64, as the shortest decimal that reads back
//         as it, with an exponent when its decimal exponent is below -4
//         or 6 or more: 0.1, 1e+06, 5e-324
//     %e  a float, with six digits after the point and an exponent:
//         1.000000e-01
//     %f  a float, with six digits after the point: 0.100000
//     %v  a value of any type above, as its own verb writes it: %d for an
//         integer, %s for a string or a []uint8, %t for a bool, %g for a
//         float
//     %%  a %, taking no argument
//
// A float is written as Go's fmt writes it, NaN as NaN, the infinities as
// +Inf and -Inf, and negative zero with its sign, -0; std/conv's
// append_float writes the same, with any precision. There are no widths,
// precisions or flags. An untyped constant argument
// is an int64, and a character literal a uint8. No verb takes a view of
// any other type, a []int64 or a []string, though one can be passed: it
// is written as a verb given a value it does not take, %!v([]int64),
// with its type and no value.
//
// When the compiler knows format, a literal or an immutable variable
// holding one, it checks each verb against its argument, and every
// mistake is a compile error. Otherwise each mistake is written into the
// output where it happens, as Go's fmt writes it, never undefined:
//
//     %!d(string=hi)          a verb given a value it does not take, or
//                             a verb printf does not know: the verb,
//                             then the value's type and its %v
//     %!d(MISSING)            a verb with no argument left
//     %!(EXTRA int64=5, ...)  after the output, arguments no verb took
//     %!(NOVERB)              a % that ends the format
//
// Like print, it writes through C's stdout, so the output of the two
// interleaves in the order of the calls, and it reports no error.
pub func printf(format string, args ...any) {
    cprintf(format, args...)
}

// cprintf is printf's body, in C: tack_fmt_printf in printf.c.
extern("tack_fmt_printf") func cprintf(format string, args ...any)

// write writes the bytes of b to standard output exactly, each as it is,
// 0 and 0xff included, and nothing else: no newline, no formatting. A
// view of no bytes writes nothing. b is a view, so a list[uint8], an
// array or a slice of uint8, or a string's bytes, []uint8(s), can be
// passed. Like print and printf, it writes through C's stdout, so the
// output of the three interleaves in the order of the calls, and it
// reports no error.
pub func write(b []uint8) {
    cwrite(b)
}

// cwrite is write's body, in C: tack_fmt_write in write.c.
extern("tack_fmt_write") func cwrite(b []uint8)

// eprintln writes s to standard error exactly as given, then a newline,
// as println does to standard output, and through the same C as eprintf:
// eprintln("") writes the newline alone. It reports no error.
pub func eprintln(s string) {
    eprintf("%s\n", s)
}

// eprintf writes format with its verbs replaced by args to standard
// error, exactly as printf writes it to standard output: the same verbs,
// the same checking of a format the compiler knows, and the same markers
// for the mistakes of one it does not. It flushes C's stdout, writes
// through C's stderr, then flushes it, so the output of it, eprintln and
// ewrite interleaves in the order of the calls, with standard output's
// too where both streams reach one place, and it reports no error.
pub func eprintf(format string, args ...any) {
    ceprintf(format, args...)
}

// ceprintf is eprintf's body, in C: tack_fmt_eprintf in printf.c.
extern("tack_fmt_eprintf") func ceprintf(format string, args ...any)

// ewrite writes the bytes of b to standard error exactly, each as it is,
// as write does to standard output: a view of no bytes writes nothing,
// though both streams are still flushed. It flushes C's stdout, writes
// through C's stderr, then flushes it, so the output of it, eprintln and
// eprintf interleaves in the order of the calls, with standard output's
// too where both streams reach one place, and it reports no error.
pub func ewrite(b []uint8) {
    cewrite(b)
}

// cewrite is ewrite's body, in C: tack_fmt_ewrite in write.c.
extern("tack_fmt_ewrite") func cewrite(b []uint8)
